# USERLAND (amix-dlm C2-3 v2 sec 4): an ordinary catalog entry, no kpkg
# entry, no apkg kernel-space record.  Part of the base set.
APKG_DESC=kbootset: compose, commit, mark good and roll back boot generations from the installed kernel packages (/sbin/kbootset, static).
APKG_ESSENTIAL=yes
DESC=The boot-generation composer of the amix-dlm loader: builds a generation from the installed kernel and loader packages, runs its gates, commits it to the boot store with a journal, and marks good, rolls back or picks the next boot.  apkg 0.3 calls it after a transaction that changed kernel space.  Upgrade kern30m -20 and kern46m -50 together with kbootset -22 or later (an old mobo root record on a 030-mode Z3660 is rewritten only from -22 on).  Since kern46m -50, s5 filesystems no longer mount on the 68040/68060 line: s5 is not safe with its 4 KiB pages; kernel -82 had s5 built in.  Since -23, after a boot the loader fell back from the default generation on its own, mark-good checks the default and marks it bad when its files are damaged (C2-4 v15).  Since -24 kbootset knows character major 54, /dev/paula: the paula-k30/-k46 module packages need the kabi 1.3 kernels kern30m -22 / kern46m -61 or later, which carry /dev/paula support and a /dev/audio open that fails at once (EIO) when Paula never interrupts instead of hanging the box.  Upgrade it with loader-amix -10: its loaders read the boot partition into fast memory where the partition (its DosEnvec Mask and BufMemType) allows, instead of always into chip memory.  Since -25 `kbootset major NAME` prints a module's majors from the registry, and the manual page kbootset(1M) is installed in /opt/amix/man.  Since -27 `kbootset major -l` lists the whole registry.  Since -29 the package owns /dev/kinfo (character 53, 0), the booted kernel's identity node, which the installer wrote before.  Since -30 kbootset knows character major 48, /dev/random and /dev/urandom: the random-k30/-k46 module packages need it, with kernels cut from a registry that has c 48.  Since -31 kbootset composes a demand-class filesystem module (cdfs), which a kabi 1.4 kernel loads at the first mount of its type.  Since -32 `kbootset cards` lists the disk controllers the running kernel drives with their match keys, and `kbootset cards /dev/dsk/cNdMsK` prints the key of that disk's controller, for an installer.  Since -33 kbootset knows character major 55, /dev/zz9ksvcN: the zz9ksvc-k30/-k46 module packages need it, with kernels cut from a registry that has c 55.  Since -34 kbootset writes the major registry into each module kernel's generation; kernels with core patch 0051 (kern30m -68, kern46m -56 and later) read a module's majors there and need kbootset -34 or later, and `kbootset check` reports a generation whose table differs from the registry.  Since -36 `kbootset inquiry <card> <unit>` and `kbootset cards --probe` ask each unit its type (disk, CD) with one read-only SCSI INQUIRY through /dev/scsi before anything reads it, and a gate-1 refusal for a changed file names the reinstall (apkg -f install <name>).  Since -35 `kbootset cards <disk>` reads the controller number of a cNdMsK name in hex, as the stock /dev/dsk names write it: it takes /dev/dsk/ced0s1 and gives card 2's key for c10-c17 (earlier it refused ca-cf and named card 1 there), and the card list prints c8-cf, c10-c17, c18-c1f.  Statically linked, GCC 14; MIT.
